				\begin{document}$$X_{c} = \frac{{w\uptheta _{c - 20} }}{{\sum\limits_{r = 1}^{20} {f_{r} + w\sum\limits_{j = 1}^{\uplambda } {\uptheta _{j} } } }}\,,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,(21 < c < 20 +\uplambda )$$\end{document}Xc=wθc-20∑r=120fr+w∑j=1λθj,(21<c<20+λ)where w is the weighting factor for the sequence-order effect and is set to w = 0.05 in antiaging-FL.

In the GAAC encoding, the 20 amino acid types are further categorized into five classes according to their physicochemical properties, e.g. hydrophobicity, charge and molecular size [68]. The five classes include the aliphatic group (g1: GAVLMI), aromatic group (g2: FYW), positive charge group (g3: KRH), negative charged group (g4: DE) and uncharged group (g5: STCPNQ). GAAC descriptor is the frequency of each amino acid group, which is defined as:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$N(g_{t} ) = \,\sum {N(t)},\qquad t \in g\ f(g) = \frac{N (g)}{N}, \quad g \in \{g1, g2, g3, g4, g5\}$$\end{document}N(gt)=∑N(t),t∈gf(g)=N(g)N,g∈{g1,g2,g3,g4,g5}

The Enhanced GAAC (EGAAC) is also for the first time proposed in this work. It calculates GAAC in windows of fixed length (default is 5) continuously sliding from the N- to C-terminal of each peptide and is usually applied to peptides with an equal length.\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$f(g,\,\,win) = \frac{N(g,\,win)}{{N(win)}},\qquad g{ \in }\,\{ g1,g2,g3,g4,g5\} ,\qquad win\,{ \in }\,\{ window1,\,window2,\,...,\,window17\}$$\end{document}f(g,win)=N(g,win)N(win),g∈{g1,g2,g3,g4,g5},win∈{window1,window2,...,window17}

The Grouped Di-Peptide Composition encoding is another variation of the DPC descriptor. It is composed of a total of 25 descriptors that are defined as:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$f(r,s)\, = \,\frac{{N_{rs} }}{N - 1},\qquad r,\,s\, { \in }\,\{ g1,\,g2,\,g3,g4,g5\}$$\end{document}f(r,s)=NrsN-1,r,s∈{g1,g2,g3,g4,g5}

The Grouped Tri-Peptide Composition encoding is also a variation of TPC descriptor, which generates 125 descriptors, defined as:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$f(r,s,t)\, = \,\frac{{N_{rst} }}{N - 2},\qquad r,\,s,\,t\,{ \in }\,\{ g1,\,g2,\,g3,g4,g5\}$$\end{document}f(r,s,t)=NrstN-2,r,s,t∈{g1,g2,g3,g4,g5}

Taking the hydrophobicity attribute as an example, all amino acids are divided into three groups: polar, neutral and hydrophobic. The Composition descriptor consists of three values: the global compositions (percentage) of polar, neutral and hydrophobic residues of the protein. The Composition descriptor can be calculated as follows:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$C(r) = \frac{N(r)}{N}\,,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,r { \in }\,\{ polar,neutral,hydrophobic\}$$\end{document}C(r)=N(r)N,r∈{polar,neutral,hydrophobic}

The Transition descriptor T also consists of three values [69, 70]: A transition from the polar group to the neutral group is the percentage frequency with which a polar residue is followed by a neutral residue or a neutral residue by a polar residue. Transitions between the neutral group and the hydrophobic group and those between the hydrophobic group and the polar group are defined in a similar way. The transition descriptor can then be calculated as:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$T(r,s) = \frac{N(r,s) + N(s,r)}{{N - 1}},\,\,\,\,\,r,s \in\,\{ (polar,neutral),(neutral,hydrophobic),(hydrophobic,polar)\}$$\end{document}T(r,s)=N(r,s)+N(s,r)N-1,r,s∈{(polar,neutral),(neutral,hydrophobic),(hydrophobic,polar)}

In this work, we employed six metrics to evaluate the performance of predictive models, including Sensitivity (SE), Specificity (SP), Accuracy (ACC), Precision, Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC), and F1-score. These metrics are calculated using the following formulas:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\left\{\begin{array}{c} Precision = \frac{TP}{TP + FP}\\ Sensitivity= \frac{TP}{TP + FN} \\ Specificity = \frac{TN}{TN + FP}\\ Acc = \frac{TP + TN}{FP + FN + TP + TN}\\ F1\ score = 2 \times \frac{Precision \times Sensitivity}{Precision + Sensitivity}\\ MCC = \frac{TP \times TN - FP \times FN}{\sqrt{(TN + FN) (TN + FP) (TP + FN) (TP + FP)}}\end{array}\right.$$\end{document}Precision=TPTP+FPSensitivity=TPTP+FNSpecificity=TNTN+FPAcc=TP+TNFP+FN+TP+TNF1score=2×Precision×SensitivityPrecision+SensitivityMCC=TP×TN-FP×FN(TN+FN)(TN+FP)(TP+FN)(TP+FP)where TP, TN, FP, and FN denote the counts of true positives, true negatives, false positives, and false negatives, respectively. SE measures the ability to predict positives, SP measures the ability to predict negatives, and Precision calculates the proportion of true positives among all positive predictions. The F1-score is the harmonic mean of Precision and Sensitivity. ACC and MCC are utilized to assess the overall performance of the predictive model.
